# Preserve Comments in Unchanged Code

When editing files, do not remove or rewrite explanatory comments that belong to code you are not intentionally changing.

## Requirements

- Leave existing comments intact in regions of the file that are outside the scope of the requested change.
- Do not "clean up" or strip comments as part of unrelated refactors, formatting passes, or copy-paste replacements.
- If a large block must be replaced, preserve comments that still apply to the surrounding or replacement logic.
- Only remove or update a comment when you are deliberately changing the behavior, API, or intent that comment describes.

## When editing

- Prefer minimal, targeted diffs — change only the lines needed for the task.
- When using search-and-replace or rewriting a section, re-include nearby comments unless they are obsolete.
- If a comment becomes inaccurate because of your change, update it; do not delete it without reason.

## Examples

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — removed a comment while fixing unrelated logic below
const timeout = 5000;

// ✅ GOOD — comment kept; only the changed line was edited
// Retry after transient network failures
const timeout = 5000;
```

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — whole function rewritten and explanatory comments dropped
function parseConfig(raw: string) {
  return JSON.parse(raw);
}

// ✅ GOOD — behavior updated; still-useful comment retained or revised
// Accepts JSON strings from env vars and legacy config files
function parseConfig(raw: string) {
  return JSON.parse(raw.trim());
}
```
